1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,285
The model remembers nothing between
requests.
So on every turn, Claude Code sends the
whole context again: the system prompt,
project context, and every prior message
and tool result.

2
00:00:12,285 --> 00:00:25,855
Prompt caching keeps this cheap.
The unchanged start, called the prefix, is
read from the cache.
That costs one tenth of the input price on
most models, and one twentieth on Opus and
Sonnet five point five.

3
00:00:25,855 --> 00:00:44,347
The cache expires after inactivity, and
each hit resets the timer.
On a subscription within your plan, it
lasts one hour.
With an API key, a cloud provider or usage
credits, it lasts five minutes.
After a longer break, the whole history is
processed again, slower and pricier.

4
00:00:44,347 --> 00:01:02,338
Near the context limit, Claude Code
compacts the history into a summary.
This is cheap while the cache is warm, and
most expensive after it expires.
The automatic pass can run mid-task and
must guess what matters.
So compact at natural breaks, and clear
between unrelated tasks.

5
00:01:02,338 --> 00:01:14,975
An automatic handoff waits for your
threshold and a natural break.
It writes a summary of what was verified,
what failed, and the one next step.
Then it clears the conversation and
continues fresh.

6
00:01:14,975 --> 00:01:31,707
Keeping the cache warm sends a tiny
request shortly before expiry.
It reads the cache and resets the timer.
But each refresh is billed as a cache read
of the whole conversation.
So it pays off only for long sessions and
breaks just past the cache lifetime.
